.TH XRAY-GATEWAY-SETUP 8 "2026-09-04" "xray-gateway 1.7.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
xray\-gateway\-setup \- detect the network layout for xray\-gateway
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xray\-gateway\-setup
.RI [ --detect-only " | " --dry-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Works out how the gateway is wired into the network and records the result in
.IR /opt/xray-proxy/config/network.conf ,
which
.BR iptables.sh (8)
and the routing scripts read.
.PP
Two layouts are recognised:
.TP
.B inline
Two network ports: the gateway sits between the ISP and the router.
.B WAN_IF
faces the ISP and is where the gateway masquerades;
.B LAN_IF
faces the router and receives forwarded traffic. Here
.B ROUTER_IP
holds the gateway's own address on the LAN link, because downstream the gateway
.I is
the router.
.TP
.B loop
One network port: the gateway sits inside the router's LAN and the router
performs the final NAT.
.B ROUTER_IP
holds the address of that upstream router.
.PP
The port carrying the default route is taken as the WAN side. For the LAN side
the first other physical port with a carrier is used, wired ports being
preferred over wireless ones, which on this hardware serve as out-of-band
management. When more than one candidate exists the choice is reported so it can
be checked.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-detect\-only
Write the file only if no configuration exists yet; leave an existing one alone.
Used by the package's post-installation script.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be detected and written, and change nothing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/xray-proxy/config/network.conf
Layout and interface names. Safe to edit by hand; restart
.B xray-proxy
afterwards.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Non-zero if no physical network interface could be found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1)
